#  Vertex AI SRE Playbook: Unified Multi-Project Alerting for Gemini 2.5 Pro

This document serves as the comprehensive, step-by-step engineering guide for configuring a **Unified Metrics Scope Alerting Suite** for Google Cloud Vertex AI.

By utilizing a central "Scoping Project," this architecture allows you to write a single PromQL alert policy that dynamically monitors all sub-projects (e.g., your 10 initiative projects) simultaneously. When an alert fires, it automatically identifies the specific failing project and model, routing actionable context directly to your on-call engineers.

---

##  Phase 1: Architecture & Prerequisites

Before creating individual alerts, you must configure your Google Cloud environment to support multi-project observability.

### 1. Configure the Metrics Scope (The "Unified" Project)

1. Navigate to the **Google Cloud Console** and select your primary "Unified Initiative" project (the central hub).
2. Go to **Monitoring > Settings**.
3. Under the **Metrics Scope** tab, click **Add GCP Projects**.
4. Select all 10 sub-projects that host your Gemini models.
5. *Result:* Your central project can now read metrics from all attached projects. All alerts created in this project will automatically evaluate data across the entire scope.

### 2. The Dynamic Grouping Logic (PromQL)

To make one alert work for multiple projects without mixing their data together, we use PromQL's grouping functions.

* ❌ **Bad (Hardcoded):** `filter resource_container="project1"`
* ❌ **Bad (Aggregated):** `sum(rate(...))` -> *This blends all 10 projects into one giant metric.*
* ✅ **Good (Dynamic):** `sum by (resource_container, model_user_id) (rate(...))` -> *This evaluates each project independently and creates a separate incident for the specific project that breaches the threshold.*

---

## ⚙️ Phase 2: Alert Creation Protocol (UI Steps)

For every alert listed in Phase 3, follow this exact creation sequence in the Google Cloud Console:

1. Go to **Monitoring > Alerting > + Create Policy**.
2. Click on **Select a Metric**, then click the **<> PromQL** button (or MQL/PromQL tab) to open the code editor.
3. Paste the provided **PromQL Query**.
4. Set the **Condition Type** to `Threshold`.
5. Set the **Alert trigger** to `Any time series violates`.
6. Enter the designated **Threshold value** (e.g., `0.05` for 5%).
7. Under **Notifications and name**, configure your notification channels (Slack, PagerDuty, Email).
8. Paste the provided **Documentation Template** into the Documentation field.
9. Enter the **Alert Policy Name** and click **Create Policy**.

