## 💡 Best Practices & Expert Recommendations

### 1. Variables in Notification Subject Lines

While Google Cloud Alerting restricts the use of variables directly in the static `display_name` of the policy, **Incident Titles** are dynamically generated by GCP.
When an alert fires via Email or Slack, Google will automatically append the grouping resource to the title.

* *Example Email Subject:* `Incident opened for Unified | 429 Quota Throttling > 2% | Gemini-2.5-Pro on project prj-initiative-app1`
* Because you heavily utilized `${resource.labels.project_id}` in the Markdown documentation body, the on-call engineer will immediately see the project name in bold at the top of the message payload.

### 2. Handling Division by Zero (PromQL Safety)

You might wonder: *"What happens to the Spillover ratio if Dedicated traffic is exactly 0?"* PromQL handles this elegantly. If the denominator evaluates to zero, PromQL drops the data point rather than throwing an infinity error. This prevents false-positive alerts during periods of zero traffic.

### 3. Notification Routing Strategy

Do not send all these alerts to the same channel. Use different Notification Channels based on the alert type:

* **PagerDuty / Critical Slack Channel:** 5xx Errors, Total Non-200. *(Requires immediate engineer intervention).*
* **SRE / DevOps Slack Channel:** 429 Errors, 499 Errors. *(Requires investigation into app behavior).*
* **FinOps / Billing Email:** Spillover Ratio, GSU Burndown. *(Capacity planning and cost discussions, usually not a 3 AM emergency).*
